// Cut from ITER.260822.05/AP3, an hour after the rule it fixes was declared working.
//
// TS.260821.08 built `an-attestation-is-not-self-issued` and it passed four tests. Then the
// iteration that built it closed cleanly, and it should not have: every phase was worked by
// agent:principal-engineer, and that role declares never-for-own "implement".
//
// It closed because `praxis close` records `by="agent:praxis"` — the TOOL — and a signer
// taken from git. Neither is the agent that did the work, so the rule compared two identities
// that can never be equal. Correct rule, no reachable path, shipped green.
//
// Underneath that is the thing this frame is actually about: the tool cannot distinguish a
// human running it from an agent running it under that human's git identity, and every
// signature in this record rests on that distinction.

thin-slice "TS.260821.09" {
    slug "an-attestation-the-tool-does-not-supply"
    title "Refuse a close whose attester the tool supplied rather than a person"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.claim-settlement"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    command "close"
    actor "whoever is accountable for the work being done"
    produces-event "AttestationRecorded" "UnattestedCloseRefused"
    needs-read-model "what-this-iteration-claims-and-has-shown"

    trigger "an iteration closes and the only identity on the close is the one the tool wrote"
    outcome "a close carries an attester that was named rather than defaulted, and one that names no distinct reviewer is refused"

    scenario #"""
        Given an iteration whose phases were all worked by one agent identity
        When it is closed with no attester named
        Then the close is REFUSED — because a default is not an attestation, and a signature the
             tool supplied proves that the tool ran, which nobody doubted.
        """#

    step "1" does="`praxis close` requires the attester to be named, and never defaults it"
    step "2" does="the attester is written into the record as its own fact, distinct from the trail's `by`"
    step "3" does="refuse when no attester is distinct from every identity that worked a covered phase"
    step "4" does="an-attestation-is-not-self-issued compares against the NAMED attester, which is what makes it reachable"
    step "5" does="the trail keeps recording the tool as `by`, because what ran and who attested are different facts and conflating them is the bug"

    excludes "making attestation unforgeable. An agent can type a human's name and this cannot \
        stop it. What changes is that doing so is an ACT rather than a default, which is the whole \
        difference between S3 and a gate — and pretending otherwise would be the root cause again"
    excludes "identity verification, signing keys, or any notion of who a person really is. The \
        record compares strings, and an engine that adjudicates identity has an opinion about employment"
    excludes "requiring a human. An agent MAY attest another agent's work — the rule is about \
        self-attestation, not about species"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="closing is something somebody does, and the skill says who"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="a close with no named attester is refused, and an-attestation-is-not-self-issued becomes reachable"
    layer "docs"        reaches="`who attested this` is a question the record answers with a name somebody chose to write"

    claim "C1" text="a close with no named attester is refused" \
        settled-by="a test closing with no attester, asserting a refusal, plus the CLI having no default for it"
    claim "C2" text="an-attestation-is-not-self-issued fires on the named attester, so it has a reachable path" \
        settled-by="an end-to-end test through the binary: work a phase as an identity, close as that identity, assert the refusal — not a core test, because AK1 and this finding are both defects the core could not see"
    claim "C3" text="what RAN and who ATTESTED stay separate facts" \
        settled-by="a test asserting the trail still records the tool as `by` while the attestation carries the named identity, and that they are not compared to each other"
    claim "C4" text="ITER.260822.05 is re-attested or its close is marked unattested" \
        settled-by="the record, either way — the iteration that shipped this defect does not get grandfathered past it"

    depends-on "TS.260821.08 — the rule and the role vocabulary exist before the path to reach them"

    useful-alone "the one gate this plugin describes as its strongest becomes reachable. It is \
        currently a rule that passes its tests and cannot fire in production, which is worse than \
        not having it: it reads as a guarantee"
    useful-to "the-team-building-a-digital-product"

    attacks "S2"

    cut-from "ITER.260822.05/AP3"

    principle-note "the rule was declared working because four tests passed and none of them ran \
        the binary. That is AK1 exactly, and AK1 already obliged a binary smoke test which was \
        written and which only checks that commands BUILD. A path that exists and cannot be \
        reached is the specific thing this record keeps failing to see, and it has now happened \
        three times"

    proves "an attestation is something a person chose to write"
    does-not-prove "that the person who wrote it did the review, or exists. It moves forgery from \
        the default path to a deliberate act, and that is the whole of what a record can do"
}
